  "worked_solution": "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the total number of students in Joan's class, which is $24.$ The percentage is $50%,$ and the part is the number of students that have brown eyes.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n50%=50÷100=0.5\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =50%\\times 24 \\\\  & =0.5\\times 24 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $50%$ of $24$ is $12.$ So, $12$ students in Joan's class have brown eyes.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n50%=\\frac{50}{100}=\\frac{50÷50}{100÷50}=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =50%\\times 24 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\times 24 \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{2} \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $50%$ of $24$ is $12.$ So, $12$ students in Joan's class have brown eyes.",
